first time joining us,

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:24.240
a special welcome to you as
well. It's so nice to meet you.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.065
I'm Rachael, I lead
customer marketing at Posit.

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:31.520
I like to start asking this,

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:34.775
but is this anybody's
first Data Science Hangout?

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.570
Say hi in the chat because we'd
love to welcome you in, and

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:41.720
say hello to anybody joining
for the first time too.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:46.160
But this is our open space
to chat about data science

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:48.330
leadership, questions
you're facing,

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:51.080
and getting to hear about
what's going on in the world of

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:53.220
data across
different industries.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:57.260
So we're here every Thursday
at the same time, same place.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.900
If you're watching this
recording on YouTube later,

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.870
the link to add it to your
calendar will be in the details

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:03.965
below as well.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:06.320
I said every Thursday,

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.500
but just a heads up we will
be off next week for the

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:10.550
Thanksgiving holiday.

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:13.220
But at the Hangouts,

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.160
we're all dedicated to
making this a welcoming environment

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:20.630
for everyone. Love hearing
from everybody no matter your

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:25.430
years of experience, your
titles, the languages you work

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:26.510
in, or industry.

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:30.800
It is totally OK to
just listen in here.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:35.060
Maybe you're walking your dog,
you're on your lunch break,

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:37.470
or just want to listen
in for the first one.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:41.180
But there's also ways you can
jump in and ask questions,

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:42.980
or provide your
own perspective.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:47.890
So you can jump in by
raising your hand on Zoom. And I'll

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:52.460
keep my eye out. You can
put questions in the Zoom chat,

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:56.710
and feel free to put a little star next
to it if you want me to read it.

00:01:56.710 --> 00:02:00.010
Or I can call on you
to introduce yourself and add some context.

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:03.230
And then we also
have a Slido link,

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:05.890
which Curtis will share in a
second here where you can ask

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:07.795
questions anonymously too.

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:11.870
Just before we get
started, I wanted to share

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:16.710
if you are hiring, feel
free to share any roles in

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:21.980
the chat here that's
definitely not spammy to us.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:25.835
The Biogen team
actually shared one

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:28.140
with me earlier today,

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.800
so I just wanted to share
it in the chat there.

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:35.240
One more shout out. If
anybody is in Boston,

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:41.120
Kevin and I are hosting Tidy
Tuesday meetup tonight. Yes, on a Thursday.

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:44.480
But that's at
Microsoft in Cambridge.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:47.210
So I'm just going to put
that in there as well.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:48.620
But with that,

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:51.980
I am so excited to introduce
my co-host for today,

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:56.870
Stephanie Lussier, manager
of biostatistics at Moderna.

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:59.660
And Stephanie, I'd love to
have you introduce yourself,

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:02.870
and share a little bit
about your role and also something

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:04.460
you like to do outside of work.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:05.330
STEPHANIE LUSSIER: Sure.

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:10.650
So thank you for the
brief introduction. As you said,

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:14.450
I'm currently a manager
of biostatistics at Moderna,

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:17.480
and I sit in our specialty
data analytics team.

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:21.020
So a little bit
about my background.

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:23.110
I graduated with a
master's in biostats

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:28.370
in 2017, and then
proceeded to work at a CRO,

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:31.800
a contract research
organization for five years.

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:34.330
And then during that time,

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:37.200
I had what some might call
a little bit more of a

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.390
traditional statistician role

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:42.300
within the clinical
trial realm,

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:46.430
and was doing open
source projects,

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:51.600
kind of more on the side
like a 20% time kind of deal.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:53.924
And so in 2022,

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:57.270
I kind of got to the point
where I really wanted that to

00:03:57.270 --> 00:04:02.310
be a bigger part of my
role. And so that's where I found

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:04.080
my current position at Moderna.

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:08.670
And so I'm in the specialty
data analytics team.

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:12.870
And our mission kind of in
simple terms is to just use

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:16.430
clinical data with modern
open source technologies

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:20.040
to really enable
internal decision-making.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:24.870
So that's where I am now,
and happy to talk more about it.

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:25.370
And

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:27.705
outside of work,

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:31.500
I kind of collect hobbies
like Pokemon cards.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:33.810
And as a result of that,

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:37.830
I'm definitely like jack of
all trades, master of none,

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:43.170
which I'm OK with. And so
some of those hobbies include yoga,

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:45.880
that's kind of a
big one for me.

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:47.340
I've been learning
to play tennis.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:53.670
I make a lot of sourdough.
I'm learning to throw pots on the

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:54.910
wheel. And

00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:59.480
I do like to travel, and when I
travel I do like to scuba dive. So

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:03.570
I'm happy to talk about
hobbies for an entire hour.

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:08.265
I love them. I hope
everyone has awesome hobbies. Yeah.

00:05:08.265 --> 00:05:08.760
RACHEL DEMPSEY: Wait,

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:12.240
can you tell me a bit more
about what throwing pots on the wheel means.

00:05:12.825 --> 00:05:15.160
- So like using clay
to make pottery.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:18.540
And so a lot of that

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:23.470
at my skill level means making
cups and bowls that aren't very

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:25.435
symmetrical but
are supposed to be.

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:27.880
It's very relaxing.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:33.160
For me like I can't draw
at all, or paint, or anything of

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:36.175
that type, and so this is
kind of an art form that

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:38.470
I can do a little bit. [LAUGHS]

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:41.635
- I love it. That's awesome.

00:05:41.635 --> 00:05:44.620
STEPHANIE LUSSIER: Oh, I wanted
to throw in one little tidbit,

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:45.450
if you don't mind.

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:49.690
So with all due respect to past

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:51.505
data leaders,

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:55.060
I thought a little bit about
what my goal is being on the

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:57.290
Data Science Hangout today. And

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:02.380
for those that don't know, the
leaders are great but the chat

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:03.860
is where it's at.

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:07.880
And so my goal is just for
people to have fun in the chat. So

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:12.030
go crazy. It has
resources, it has debates,

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:14.513
it has fun topics.

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:18.070
And if you've never
participated in the chat

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:21.865
before, I'd encourage you
to do so if you want to.

00:06:21.865 --> 00:06:22.970
RACHEL DEMPSEY: I love it.

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:25.990
Thank you so much for that
call out to the chat. Yes,

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:28.480
the chat is always
a party. [LAUGHS]

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:33.160
I see the excitement starting
already in there. [LAUGHS]

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:34.270
Well, Stephanie,

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:38.650
while we are waiting for some
questions to come in from everybody here,

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:44.410
I know something that we talked
a bit about is becoming a

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:46.160
manager for the first time.

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:48.640
And I'm curious if
there's something that

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:52.660
you've learned or you had
to learn throughout this process

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:56.500
of becoming a manager that
you'd be able to share with us.

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:57.085
- Sure.

00:06:57.760 --> 00:06:59.920
So a lot of pieces of this.

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:06.140
In my previous role, like I
said more as a traditional statistician,

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:11.400
you work within
clinical teams. And so

00:07:12.490 --> 00:07:16.000
you end up doing some
management of statistical

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:18.330
outputs. So that
is kind of managing

00:07:19.030 --> 00:07:22.880
statisticians and programmers
on a project level.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:26.500
And I think that was
really good preparation for being an

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:28.450
actual true manager.

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:32.515
And so now I'm on a team.

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:36.820
We have seven full time
employees, and seven

00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:39.370
contractors, and

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:46.300
starting to pick up part of
the true management pieces of that.

00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:50.010
But let's see, as far
as what I've learned,

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:54.890
there's a lot of paperwork
parts of it. And so hopefully

00:07:54.890 --> 00:07:59.240
anyone becoming a manager has
some sort of mentor walking

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:00.575
them through that part of it.

00:08:01.430 --> 00:08:04.820
For example, requesting a
laptop is a pretty difficult

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:07.550
goal to achieve apparently.

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:12.170
Shout out to our awesome
digital team who helps with that.

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:17.030
So learning like those little
technical pieces of it is a

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:20.700
whole new world to just take
on slowly and ask questions.

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:22.040
I think that's the
most important thing.

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:27.770
And then as far as
actual management, I think just

00:08:27.770 --> 00:08:29.900
forming a relationship
with people,

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:35.280
working closely together,
asking them to ask questions,

00:08:36.230 --> 00:08:39.830
just really creating a
comfortable space has been

00:08:39.830 --> 00:08:40.939
really important for me.

00:08:41.525 --> 00:08:42.510
RACHEL DEMPSEY: Thank you. STEPHANIE

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:42.820
LUSSIER: Yeah.

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:44.960
- I see Bill jumped in
right away with a question,

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:47.900
and I know you said
you want me to ask it.

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:52.630
But the question was, when you graduate--

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:55.420
or what is the language that
you use in your academic

00:08:55.420 --> 00:09:00.090
program? Did you need to learn
SAS when you joined the CRO?

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:01.510
is the question. And-- STEPHANIE

00:09:01.510 --> 00:09:01.900
LUSSIER: Sure.

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:06.040
- --maybe the acronyms you will
expand on them a bit too. [CHUCKLES]

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:11.320
- Yeah. I'm doing my very
best with not over using acronyms.

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:13.645
So please catch me if I do.

00:09:14.710 --> 00:09:19.360
Yeah, so in the clinical
trial world, the main

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:23.710
statistical language that
is used right now is SAS.

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:28.300
I don't know what SAS
stands for. It's fine.

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:31.820
But it's a
statistical programming language.

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:36.100
So during my grad school, I
was lucky enough to learn both

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:39.100
SAS and R, and
even some Python.

00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:45.520
And I think that really
guided where I ended up today.

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:50.110
So SAS was taught in certain
classes that I had, and in

00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:53.450
other classes as the
professors favored R.

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:57.950
And I think the reason I really
ended up on the R path was that

00:09:57.950 --> 00:10:03.200
my main thesis project was done
in R. And so that really gave

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:07.850
me a strong foundation so that
when I started my first role,

00:10:08.690 --> 00:10:10.625
I was definitely
expected to use

00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:13.970
SAS and that more
traditional setting.

00:10:13.970 --> 00:10:18.170
But I was able to connect
with the people in my first organization

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:23.630
who did use R. And that really
kicked off thinking more

00:10:23.630 --> 00:10:25.370
about developing with R,

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:30.140
and what R in the clinical
trial space looks like.

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:31.040
RACHEL DEMPSEY: Thank you. STEPHANIE

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:31.585
LUSSIER: Yeah.

00:10:32.335 --> 00:10:32.810
-

00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:36.530
Something that you and I
had chatted a little bit about

00:10:36.530 --> 00:10:40.520
before is that you've kind
of realized like people don't want

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:41.580
to look at these standard

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:45.020
tables listings and figures.

00:10:45.020 --> 00:10:49.250
Which I just learned that's
what TLFS means when I see that

00:10:49.250 --> 00:10:51.755
listed are named in pharma.

00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:53.765
But I'm curious,

00:10:55.330 --> 00:10:59.710
in building tools for
internal decision-making,

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:03.690
what has been really helpful
for you in communicating? STEPHANIE

00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:04.465
LUSSIER: Yeah.

00:11:05.230 --> 00:11:09.760
So the fun thing too about
the TLF abbreviation is that it

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:13.370
goes by many abbreviations.
So watch out for that.

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:16.420
It can be TLF, TFL, TLG, TGL,

00:11:16.420 --> 00:11:17.590
and that all means
the same thing.

00:11:17.590 --> 00:11:20.078
- It was right on top of it
in the chat too. [LAUGHS]

00:11:21.542 --> 00:11:23.710
- And why is it like that?

00:11:23.710 --> 00:11:25.615
I think that's just
to confuse people.

00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:29.230
And I think that's a
common theme with abbreviations in

00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:33.550
clinical trials. So to
actually answer the question, yeah,

00:11:33.550 --> 00:11:37.660
a big part of my role is
creating visualizations for

00:11:37.660 --> 00:11:39.860
internal decision-making.

00:11:39.860 --> 00:11:43.735
And so we think a lot about
what is the best way to do that.

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:47.673
I think one of
the most important questions is,

00:11:48.380 --> 00:11:52.190
what is the question that
someone is trying to answer?

00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:53.665
And who is the end user?

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:56.540
And so for example,

00:11:56.540 --> 00:12:00.700
a lot of times the end user
for us is say the executive committee.

00:12:01.210 --> 00:12:03.955
And so people on the
executive committee

00:12:04.780 --> 00:12:08.420
at Moderna at least are
highly skilled professionals.

00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:13.540
They're not looking for a
green button or a red button.

00:12:13.540 --> 00:12:15.833
They want to know
what were the results?

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:19.127
And they want to see it in a

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:21.630
visually pleasing

00:12:22.570 --> 00:12:26.800
straightforward display. But they
also still want to know

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:29.260
some key statistical elements.

00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:32.500
And so that's actually part of
the reason why a lot of the

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:35.800
people on our team
have a statistics background is

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:38.350
because we are still displaying

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:43.310
confidence intervals and
risk ratios in our displays.

00:12:44.990 --> 00:12:48.970
And so that's an example
where we know the audience,

00:12:48.970 --> 00:12:51.040
and we know the questions
they're going to ask.

00:12:52.150 --> 00:12:53.785
Another example would be

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:58.540
we create say visualizations that
are going to be used by

00:12:58.540 --> 00:13:02.540
the clinical team that want to
look at data in an ongoing way.

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:06.520
And so you might think about
how well those people know this

00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:10.070
data, and they want to
know every nitty-gritty about it.

00:13:10.070 --> 00:13:13.630
And so that's where
something like interactive applications

00:13:13.630 --> 00:13:15.500
really comes into play.

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:18.160
So that as they form
their own questions

00:13:18.940 --> 00:13:21.430
they can answer
those questions.

00:13:21.430 --> 00:13:22.270
RACHEL DEMPSEY: That's great.

00:13:22.270 --> 00:13:26.995
Can you share maybe an example for
us or an example use case where

00:13:28.050 --> 00:13:33.405
people were able to answer some of
their own questions with an interactive app?

00:13:33.405 --> 00:13:35.970
STEPHANIE LUSSIER:
Sure. So I'm working--

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:39.150
one of the big projects that
I work on both internally and

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:43.410
externally right now is on
patient profiles. And so the

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:45.310
whole goal with
patient profiles

00:13:45.990 --> 00:13:50.130
is that you can look
at one particular participant in a

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:54.840
study. And the primary uses
there is really for safety.

00:13:55.660 --> 00:13:57.400
So you could look
at, say their labs,

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:00.270
basic demographics information,

00:14:01.140 --> 00:14:02.920
what we call adverse events.

00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:06.030
So any safety signals
that happen through the study.

00:14:07.270 --> 00:14:12.494
And so we had a participant
that had an unexpected

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:15.440
response in a biomarker.

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:20.910
And so they were able to
use the patient profile to look at

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:25.440
when that particular
biomarker uptick occurred,

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:29.455
what else was going on for
that particular participant.

00:14:29.455 --> 00:14:32.485
RACHEL DEMPSEY: Thank you.
I think Bill's question maybe

00:14:33.190 --> 00:14:35.260
expands a bit on
this. Let's see. STEPHANIE

00:14:35.260 --> 00:14:35.760
LUSSIER: Sure.

00:14:37.075 --> 00:14:38.350
RACHEL DEMPSEY: Was that

00:14:39.130 --> 00:14:41.770
that visualization was
that a Shiny application.

00:14:41.770 --> 00:14:42.730
I think Bill's question was,

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:45.775
have you developed Shiny-based
apps for your internal users?

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:50.290
And have you ended up using
the same interactive or static

00:14:50.290 --> 00:14:55.000
visualizations in drill-downs when
interacting with the FDA?

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:57.220
STEPHANIE LUSSIER: OK,
that's like three questions. RACHEL

00:14:57.220 --> 00:14:59.740
DEMPSEY: Yeah, we can break we can
break it up a bit, yeah. STEPHANIE

00:14:59.740 --> 00:15:00.715
LUSSIER: No,
that's OK.

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:02.280
So

00:15:03.970 --> 00:15:08.360
much to say here. So the
particular example I provided

00:15:10.420 --> 00:15:15.640
is actually all being
powered by non-Shiny interactivity.

00:15:15.640 --> 00:15:20.000
And there's been a couple of
great talks about this topic lately.

00:15:20.680 --> 00:15:21.280
[INAUDIBLE]

00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:24.550
gave a hilarious
talk at Posit Conf

00:15:25.210 --> 00:15:28.630
about this. And my
mentor, Agustin Calatroni,

00:15:28.630 --> 00:15:32.590
also had a recorded talk at
our pharma where he kind of touched

00:15:32.590 --> 00:15:33.625
on this as well.

00:15:34.660 --> 00:15:39.410
And so kind of with a judicious
use of cross talk plotly

00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:41.840
interactive tables.

00:15:42.520 --> 00:15:45.660
You can make an
interactive HTML document

00:15:47.710 --> 00:15:51.640
that has a limited but
pretty good capabilities.

00:15:53.470 --> 00:15:58.675
And so that is the current
platform for that particular patient profile.

00:15:59.800 --> 00:16:02.560
So yeah, if you're not
familiar with that setup,

00:16:02.560 --> 00:16:03.560
I do think it's powerful.

00:16:04.660 --> 00:16:09.020
In my previous company, we
didn't really have a good

00:16:09.700 --> 00:16:11.360
hosting platform,

00:16:11.360 --> 00:16:14.155
and so we were able to
really utilize that setup

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:17.610
to create interactive reports.

00:16:18.490 --> 00:16:22.600
Like at a high level
the limitation is that the

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:27.430
interactivity happens on one
level sort of, and there's

00:16:27.430 --> 00:16:29.070
no recalculation

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:33.090
happening of the data.
You're essentially filtering the data.

00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:36.810
But you can really do
a lot with that setup.

00:16:38.100 --> 00:16:39.000
So then

00:16:40.220 --> 00:16:41.900
moving to like Shiny land,

00:16:41.900 --> 00:16:44.345
it's definitely something
that our team uses.

00:16:45.500 --> 00:16:48.890
We have a variety of
different visualizations.

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:55.160
And so that decision is kind
part of what we talked about

00:16:55.160 --> 00:16:59.330
earlier when you get say
a data visualization request deciding

00:16:59.330 --> 00:17:04.069
what is the right technology.
And so if more and more

00:17:04.069 --> 00:17:08.479
exploration is the goal, then Shiny
I think is the way to go.

00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:11.375
And we definitely utilize that.

00:17:12.730 --> 00:17:17.369
And then I think the third
question is about sending that

00:17:17.369 --> 00:17:19.189
to the FDA. And

00:17:19.890 --> 00:17:23.024
that's not something that
we're doing right now.

00:17:23.710 --> 00:17:27.130
But I think probably a
lot of folks are following

00:17:28.109 --> 00:17:33.300
the R submissions group, and they've
done a pilot to look at

00:17:33.300 --> 00:17:36.480
what a Shiny app to the
FDA could look like.

00:17:36.480 --> 00:17:38.880
And they're going
to be doing more

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:41.950
utilizing the web R framework,

00:17:42.730 --> 00:17:46.305
which is a hot, hot
exciting topic right now

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:51.480
that could apply really well for
this concept of Shiny to FDA.

00:17:52.470 --> 00:17:54.080
So many answers.

00:17:54.080 --> 00:17:56.070
RACHEL DEMPSEY: Right, great job.

00:17:56.070 --> 00:17:57.030
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:58.470 --> 00:18:02.100
And I guess probably fun to
just throw it out here as well

00:18:02.100 --> 00:18:04.920
that some of the team from
the FDA is actually going to be

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:06.405
joining us at the Hangout

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:12.400
on February 1, so putting it
out there so people know as

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:16.540
well. But I see a lot of
love for cross talk in the chat.

00:18:16.540 --> 00:18:19.570
It feels like we should have
some sort of event on that at

00:18:19.570 --> 00:18:20.410
some point. [LAUGHS] STEPHANIE

00:18:20.410 --> 00:18:23.320
LUSSIER: Let's talk
event. Very awesome.

00:18:23.320 --> 00:18:24.605
RACHEL DEMPSEY: Awesome. Sita,

00:18:24.605 --> 00:18:26.570
I see you asked a
question in the chat.

00:18:26.570 --> 00:18:27.385
Do you want to jump in here?

00:18:27.955 --> 00:18:29.480
AUDIENCE: Yeah, hi, thanks.

00:18:30.220 --> 00:18:31.460
So I'm curious

00:18:32.200 --> 00:18:35.740
from a more management
perspective, how did you

00:18:35.740 --> 00:18:37.420
navigate this

00:18:38.620 --> 00:18:40.330
transition from a more

00:18:41.200 --> 00:18:44.680
day-to-day interactions with
more biostatisticians who knew

00:18:44.680 --> 00:18:48.550
the same language and were
talking the same way versus to

00:18:48.550 --> 00:18:50.545
working at Moderna where

00:18:51.310 --> 00:18:56.040
you kind of have to interface
with a whole different group of people?

00:18:56.740 --> 00:18:59.840
Say biologists,
data scientists,

00:19:00.790 --> 00:19:03.310
very diverse kind
of group of people

00:19:04.090 --> 00:19:09.100
assuming that it was less
diverse in the sense of having

00:19:09.100 --> 00:19:11.860
more biostatisticians
in your previous role.

00:19:11.860 --> 00:19:14.200
And kind of what
challenges did you have?

00:19:14.200 --> 00:19:15.775
And how did you deal with that?

00:19:16.300 --> 00:19:18.415
- OK, yeah, I love
that question.

00:19:19.550 --> 00:19:23.810
I think it's an awesome
part about the clinical trial world,

00:19:24.490 --> 00:19:29.780
is that you do get to interface
with people in so many different roles.

00:19:30.980 --> 00:19:34.630
And I would say that at
my previous position I did

00:19:34.630 --> 00:19:37.450
interface with a lot
of different types of people

00:19:37.450 --> 00:19:41.340
already. And so maybe
that was good preparation.

00:19:42.430 --> 00:19:47.170
Working with clinicians on the trial
I even used to have phone

00:19:47.170 --> 00:19:52.330
calls with people at the sites.
That was always fun to feel

00:19:52.330 --> 00:19:54.782
more a part of the trial.

00:19:56.020 --> 00:20:00.910
So I think I definitely
already had cross-functional area training.

00:20:02.110 --> 00:20:06.550
So then moving to Moderna,
I think part of the

00:20:06.550 --> 00:20:10.570
difference is the group that we're
in now is very much like

00:20:10.570 --> 00:20:11.860
a support team.

00:20:12.790 --> 00:20:14.400
So we have presents

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:17.170
on every study.

00:20:17.170 --> 00:20:21.130
And our role is a little
bit like we're here to help you,

00:20:21.130 --> 00:20:24.160
how do we help?
Does this help you?

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:29.080
And so gaining a pretty
good understanding of, what would

00:20:29.080 --> 00:20:34.120
help people is really important so
that we can deliver tools that

00:20:34.840 --> 00:20:35.725
are useful.

00:20:38.240 --> 00:20:40.200
Did that answer your
question a little bit?

00:20:40.200 --> 00:20:41.060
AUDIENCE: Yes,
thank you so much.

00:20:42.365 --> 00:20:43.530
STEPHANIE LUSSIER:
Thank you, yeah.

00:20:43.530 --> 00:20:46.290
- Ross, I think you had
a question in the chat,

00:20:46.290 --> 00:20:47.330
do you want to
jump in here next?

00:20:47.955 --> 00:20:49.090
AUDIENCE: Sure, thanks.

00:20:50.150 --> 00:20:54.490
I similar to you wound up
sending reports up and stuff,

00:20:54.490 --> 00:20:58.300
and sometimes the feedback that I'll get
is good and sometimes it's like, eh.

00:20:58.300 --> 00:21:00.980
What's one thing that the
leadership does that's good,

00:21:00.980 --> 00:21:03.590
that's helpful to you, that

00:21:04.390 --> 00:21:06.490
gets them the report
that they actually want?

00:21:07.375 --> 00:21:09.040
- Yeah, I love that.

00:21:10.090 --> 00:21:13.915
So like what is good feedback
and what is less helpful feedback?

00:21:18.510 --> 00:21:19.185
I'm thinking,

00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:21.980
good feedback

00:21:24.380 --> 00:21:30.590
is for them to tell us how and why

00:21:31.370 --> 00:21:33.650
a report was or wasn't useful.

00:21:35.040 --> 00:21:38.350
So that we can then apply
it to future reports.

00:21:40.010 --> 00:21:44.600
And so the why I think is
important. And maybe you have

00:21:44.600 --> 00:21:45.680
to ask the why

00:21:46.700 --> 00:21:48.360
because if they just say

00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:50.540
this didn't help,

00:21:52.740 --> 00:21:54.013
that's not useful.

00:21:54.700 --> 00:21:57.690
So getting like a better
understanding of what they were

00:21:57.690 --> 00:21:59.320
looking for, maybe

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:04.920
an understanding of our report
answers the question that they

00:22:04.920 --> 00:22:06.580
asked, hopefully.

00:22:06.580 --> 00:22:11.100
But if that question changed
over time and that's why the

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:12.690
report is no longer helpful,

00:22:13.710 --> 00:22:17.490
a little bit of insight
into how that question might have

00:22:17.490 --> 00:22:21.900
changed or why that question
might have changed helps us predict

00:22:22.620 --> 00:22:23.380
in the future

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:28.920
how questions might change or
how we can provide supplemental

00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:32.970
materials that might
answer those changing questions.

00:22:36.270 --> 00:22:41.250
So yeah, I think the takeaway on
that is it's OK to turn it

00:22:41.250 --> 00:22:44.610
into a conversation and probe a
little bit more so that you can

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:48.885
move forward and
improve for the future.

00:22:49.665 --> 00:22:51.310
AUDIENCE: OK, thank you.

00:22:51.310 --> 00:22:51.830
STEPHANIE LUSSIER: Yeah.

00:22:52.770 --> 00:22:55.280
Did you have any thoughts
you wanted to add, Ross?

00:22:57.000 --> 00:22:58.465
AUDIENCE: I'll just
make it really quick. STEPHANIE

00:22:58.465 --> 00:22:59.020
LUSSIER: Yeah.

00:22:59.020 --> 00:23:01.030
AUDIENCE: I have a
lot of prior careers,

00:23:01.030 --> 00:23:03.055
one of which I'm actually
a professional counselor

00:23:04.780 --> 00:23:05.920
with a master's in
the whole thing.

00:23:05.920 --> 00:23:08.770
So to me listening is really,
really, really critical.

00:23:09.790 --> 00:23:11.690
I think it's almost more
important than the analysis.

00:23:11.690 --> 00:23:15.170
So just make sure that I
really truly understand what the question is.

00:23:15.170 --> 00:23:16.990
And if I can give a
professional counselor tip for

00:23:16.990 --> 00:23:19.690
just a second, also
find out what it is not

00:23:20.800 --> 00:23:22.210
because when you
find out what it is,

00:23:22.210 --> 00:23:24.190
you might go four miles
down that path and find, oh,

00:23:24.190 --> 00:23:26.260
it's actually something
slightly different.

00:23:26.260 --> 00:23:28.150
I was working on that.
No, we don't want that.

00:23:28.150 --> 00:23:31.300
Is it this and not that?
And once you do that,

00:23:31.300 --> 00:23:33.355
a lot of things clear up,
and then it's easy to move forward.

00:23:34.906 --> 00:23:38.910
- Awesome. Yeah, I previously
did a lot of work on

00:23:38.910 --> 00:23:40.035
manuscript writing.

00:23:41.080 --> 00:23:45.270
And so in traditional clinical
trials, you have your endpoints

00:23:46.650 --> 00:23:51.120
that are pre-written, and you
answer those endpoints that are

00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:55.230
pre-specified. And sometimes
in a more exploratory

00:23:55.230 --> 00:23:57.540
biomarker manuscript setting,

00:23:58.800 --> 00:24:00.750
there can be a lot
of back and forth.

00:24:00.750 --> 00:24:04.210
Like you're talking about
with questions changing over time.

00:24:04.210 --> 00:24:07.650
And so keeping the
conversation flowing was always

00:24:08.820 --> 00:24:11.070
important to really
narrow the scope,

00:24:11.070 --> 00:24:15.015
and try to find the
answers that they were looking for.

00:24:15.770 --> 00:24:16.150
RACHEL DEMPSEY:

00:24:16.150 --> 00:24:19.810
I see a lot of great thoughts
on this topic too in the chat.

00:24:19.810 --> 00:24:20.580
I don't know if, Catherine,

00:24:20.580 --> 00:24:23.130
you wanted to share
your thoughts here too.

00:24:23.130 --> 00:24:23.775
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:24:24.930 --> 00:24:25.650
I was just--

00:24:25.650 --> 00:24:28.440
I'll kind of repeat what I
said in the chat is one of the

00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:31.920
things that I learned
to ask for is like,

00:24:32.760 --> 00:24:36.180
what questions are my
leadership being asked that led

00:24:36.180 --> 00:24:38.640
them to ask me to do something?

00:24:38.640 --> 00:24:41.640
Because how they're
interpreting their own--

00:24:41.640 --> 00:24:43.330
the questions that
they're being asked.

00:24:43.330 --> 00:24:46.770
And then there's three
levels of information distillation

00:24:46.770 --> 00:24:49.830
before they ask you
for a piece of math,

00:24:49.830 --> 00:24:52.430
or a piece of reporting,
or a visualization.

00:24:53.670 --> 00:24:56.670
And so then you make the thing
that you think they were asking for,

00:24:56.670 --> 00:24:59.940
but it doesn't really meet
the ask that their leadership asked

00:24:59.940 --> 00:25:03.160
them for. And so there's a
little bit of a cycle there.

00:25:03.160 --> 00:25:06.000
And so getting plugged into
meetings where you can listen

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:09.660
to how your leadership talks
about these kinds of things,

00:25:09.660 --> 00:25:12.630
or being forwarded emails or
things like that can really

00:25:12.630 --> 00:25:16.980
help you get a broader
picture of where your organization fits

00:25:16.980 --> 00:25:20.640
within the broader thing. So
OK, this is how the reports

00:25:20.640 --> 00:25:24.780
that I deliver are used by
these other teams to get more

00:25:24.780 --> 00:25:25.683
of a sense of like, OK,

00:25:26.530 --> 00:25:30.240
what information is actually
important to the ultimate

00:25:30.240 --> 00:25:31.410
decision-making?

00:25:32.082 --> 00:25:37.160
- Yeah, one of the comments
that you touched on, Catherine,

00:25:37.160 --> 00:25:40.130
is something that we kind
of prioritize in our team.

00:25:40.130 --> 00:25:43.115
And that's asking to
get invited to the meetings

00:25:44.120 --> 00:25:44.620
to

00:25:45.350 --> 00:25:49.010
parse down the amount
of distillation that occurs.

00:25:50.430 --> 00:25:54.440
And so no one wants a
calendar full of meetings when there's

00:25:54.440 --> 00:25:58.250
lots of deep work to do.
But when their important

00:25:58.970 --> 00:26:00.530
asking to get invited,

00:26:00.530 --> 00:26:03.455
so you can hear the
whole conversation instead of the summary.

00:26:05.216 --> 00:26:08.270
RACHEL DEMPSEY: Lisa,
I see you had some nice

00:26:09.170 --> 00:26:11.330
feedback on this as
well. Do you want to--

00:26:11.330 --> 00:26:12.470
do you want to share that?

00:26:13.940 --> 00:26:16.930
- I'm sorry, feel like
I wrote a lot today.

00:26:16.930 --> 00:26:19.860
So are you talking about
just the comparison to

00:26:20.730 --> 00:26:22.110
feedback from students or-- RACHEL

00:26:22.110 --> 00:26:23.760
DEMPSEY: Yeah. Yeah,
the [AUDIO OUT]

00:26:23.760 --> 00:26:25.080
sense of teaching, yeah.

00:26:25.080 --> 00:26:27.090
- Yeah. No, I was just more--

00:26:27.090 --> 00:26:30.960
I don't think I have much
to add besides that it just sort

00:26:30.960 --> 00:26:34.300
of triggered in my mind that
it reminded me of teaching.

00:26:34.300 --> 00:26:36.304
And when I get these responses,

00:26:37.150 --> 00:26:39.645
especially when I first started
teaching that they would be like,

00:26:40.530 --> 00:26:43.965
you're awful, or you're
really nice. And I was like,

00:26:44.740 --> 00:26:47.010
oh, I feel really lovely
that you think I'm nice,

00:26:47.010 --> 00:26:49.440
or I feel terrible that
you think I'm awful. But

00:26:50.190 --> 00:26:51.645
neither of those
really help me.

00:26:53.010 --> 00:26:56.380
So I always ask them to
be specific as possible.

00:26:57.420 --> 00:26:58.990
But I've never
thought of asking

00:26:59.940 --> 00:27:01.935
someone at work
for that specific

00:27:02.640 --> 00:27:04.170
feedback. So that was

00:27:05.640 --> 00:27:08.325
a nice connection is all
I was saying. [CHUCKLES] RACHEL

00:27:08.325 --> 00:27:09.690
DEMPSEY: Thank you
for sharing. Yeah,

00:27:09.690 --> 00:27:13.290
I'm trying to call out some
of the chat comments as well

00:27:13.290 --> 00:27:15.190
because I know when people
watch the recording,

00:27:15.190 --> 00:27:17.670
they don't get the advantage
of getting to see all these

00:27:17.670 --> 00:27:19.035
amazing comments too.

00:27:20.760 --> 00:27:21.930
I know--

00:27:21.930 --> 00:27:22.770
let's see, Adrian,

00:27:22.770 --> 00:27:25.660
you asked a question in
the chat a bit earlier.

00:27:25.660 --> 00:27:26.640
Do you to jump in?

00:27:27.140 --> 00:27:28.915
- Yeah, let me see if I can

00:27:29.890 --> 00:27:32.860
recall it and restate
it pretty clearly.

00:27:34.560 --> 00:27:36.960
I'm interested in, Stephanie,

00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:40.860
at Moderna and with the
profile of work you're doing.

00:27:40.860 --> 00:27:43.560
How recyclable is

00:27:44.490 --> 00:27:46.870
sort of those analysis
or those reports?

00:27:46.870 --> 00:27:49.360
And if you want
a bit of context,

00:27:50.980 --> 00:27:54.730
I spent a lot of time
trying to not do anything multiple times.

00:27:54.730 --> 00:27:56.365
So I'm curious if

00:27:57.310 --> 00:28:00.760
the biostats area or at least
with your team if there is a

00:28:00.760 --> 00:28:04.375
lot of opportunity to library your

00:28:05.140 --> 00:28:07.420
sort of starting points
or what have you.

00:28:08.420 --> 00:28:10.525
- Yes, great question.

00:28:11.980 --> 00:28:15.700
The short, very short answer
is yes. There's a lot of

00:28:15.700 --> 00:28:17.560
recyclability. And

00:28:18.850 --> 00:28:24.310
I think a lot of similar
teams as well as mine are working

00:28:24.310 --> 00:28:28.900
on creating internal
packages that help with that

00:28:28.900 --> 00:28:30.430
recyclability.

00:28:30.430 --> 00:28:34.810
But something like it's kind
of been on my mind lately is

00:28:34.810 --> 00:28:39.490
making sure that when
you're creating functions, modules, whatever,

00:28:40.870 --> 00:28:44.650
you're still allowing for
customization. Because

00:28:45.430 --> 00:28:47.550
at least in my experience

00:28:48.260 --> 00:28:52.060
what people in clinical
teams they want to add stuff.

00:28:52.060 --> 00:28:54.820
They want to say, can
we look at this too?

00:28:54.820 --> 00:28:56.070
Can you add this facet?

00:28:58.060 --> 00:28:58.560
Even

00:28:59.560 --> 00:29:05.170
little details, and so as
we go on making progress

00:29:06.250 --> 00:29:10.050
in creating an internal package system.

00:29:10.970 --> 00:29:13.950
That's something that we're trying
to keep in mind is

00:29:14.690 --> 00:29:18.560
finding that sweet spot
between out of the box, boom,

00:29:18.560 --> 00:29:21.710
here you go. I made you
this plot, and allowing for

00:29:23.000 --> 00:29:24.170
customization.

00:29:25.930 --> 00:29:27.531
Thumbs up. Awesome. [LAUGHS]

00:29:29.375 --> 00:29:33.980
- I want to jump to a few
of the anonymous questions too. And one was,

00:29:35.330 --> 00:29:39.170
do you have any advice for
a recent graduate trying to get

00:29:39.170 --> 00:29:42.740
her foot in the door? It's
a bit hard to do currently.

00:29:42.740 --> 00:29:43.490
- OK.

00:29:45.110 --> 00:29:46.355
We're all with you.

00:29:47.000 --> 00:29:48.020
It's not easy.

00:29:48.980 --> 00:29:49.480
My

00:29:50.090 --> 00:29:55.150
job search to switch jobs took
what felt like a long time.

00:29:55.150 --> 00:29:56.840
I don't want to
put a number on it.

00:29:57.380 --> 00:30:01.310
Like I was always told
statisticians can get a new job

00:30:01.310 --> 00:30:05.040
so quickly, and I was really
picky in my situation.

00:30:05.040 --> 00:30:08.150
And so it wasn't
quick. And I did have

00:30:09.004 --> 00:30:12.260
fear surrounding that
I look back on now,

00:30:12.260 --> 00:30:13.520
and it all makes sense.

00:30:15.560 --> 00:30:19.354
But for thinking about
someone with less experience,

00:30:21.710 --> 00:30:25.490
I saw the chat going this
direction last week which is a

00:30:25.490 --> 00:30:29.070
topic that I think is
really cool to talk about is

00:30:29.810 --> 00:30:32.410
the presence of
an online GitHub

00:30:33.110 --> 00:30:37.130
repository because I think
there's arguments for and

00:30:37.130 --> 00:30:41.330
against this. But I think in
a position of being a recent

00:30:41.330 --> 00:30:45.905
grad, that's something that
I would think about doing is

00:30:46.640 --> 00:30:50.420
do some little tiny projects and
put them on GitHub just to

00:30:50.420 --> 00:30:52.300
demonstrate your
programming skills.

00:30:54.500 --> 00:30:57.920
And then in the clinical
trial space, if you're having

00:30:57.920 --> 00:31:02.360
difficulty, I would suggest
looking at contract research

00:31:02.360 --> 00:31:06.275
organizations. I learned
so much in that role.

00:31:07.410 --> 00:31:10.410
And I think they have
a lot of positions

00:31:11.330 --> 00:31:14.240
hopefully for more entry level.

00:31:16.010 --> 00:31:20.060
Like I hope I'm not making
too big of generalizations, I think

00:31:20.060 --> 00:31:23.900
sometimes the pharma side
of thing does look for more

00:31:23.900 --> 00:31:28.370
experience. And there are more
entry level associate type

00:31:28.370 --> 00:31:33.330
roles in the CRO world. So
that's something to look into

00:31:33.330 --> 00:31:34.305
and read more about.

00:31:35.310 --> 00:31:38.330
And then another thing
I would think about is

00:31:39.560 --> 00:31:44.250
I don't know exactly
your background, but the

00:31:45.120 --> 00:31:48.630
clinical trials world has a
lot of clinical trials knowledge.

00:31:49.740 --> 00:31:54.870
And so trying to find
some resources online to become

00:31:54.870 --> 00:31:57.660
familiar with what a
clinical trial looks like.

00:31:58.590 --> 00:32:01.200
We have data
standards called CDISC

00:32:01.980 --> 00:32:04.980
to do a little bit of
research about that.

00:32:05.670 --> 00:32:09.070
I think that would make you
really stand out as a candidate.

00:32:09.070 --> 00:32:10.280
RACHEL DEMPSEY: Thank you.

00:32:11.880 --> 00:32:12.380
Matt,

00:32:13.190 --> 00:32:15.900
I see you had a
question a bit earlier.

00:32:15.900 --> 00:32:18.635
Do you want to jump
in and read that one?

00:32:18.635 --> 00:32:19.520
AUDIENCE: Sure.

00:32:20.570 --> 00:32:21.770
So I was curious.

00:32:21.770 --> 00:32:24.275
You talked about some of
the dashboards you've built.

00:32:25.160 --> 00:32:28.370
And I'm just curious what's
the limiting principle for how much

00:32:28.370 --> 00:32:30.170
interactivity to give people?

00:32:30.170 --> 00:32:33.860
Because I've kind of found that
there are two sides to that

00:32:33.860 --> 00:32:36.120
continuum. One, you can
just keep building the app,

00:32:36.120 --> 00:32:38.510
and building the app,
and adding more functionality.

00:32:39.270 --> 00:32:41.130
But you want to deliver it.

00:32:41.130 --> 00:32:43.180
And then the more
content you add--

00:32:43.180 --> 00:32:45.380
the more functionality you add
to it the more possibility--

00:32:45.380 --> 00:32:48.020
someone else pointed out that
there's a possibility that

00:32:48.020 --> 00:32:51.050
people are going to
derive insights that

00:32:51.710 --> 00:32:54.740
aren't necessarily
true or meaningful.

00:32:54.740 --> 00:32:56.030
But then on the flip side,

00:32:56.030 --> 00:32:58.370
you know that the one thing
you don't implement is the thing

00:32:58.370 --> 00:33:03.140
you're going to get asked for. So
where do you draw the line on

00:33:04.070 --> 00:33:05.810
what to do and
what to leave out?

00:33:06.516 --> 00:33:08.343
STEPHANIE LUSSIER:
That's a big question.

00:33:09.070 --> 00:33:11.640
It's difficult to nail down.

00:33:12.450 --> 00:33:12.950
I think

00:33:13.720 --> 00:33:18.390
in my particular realm
of things, you can--

00:33:18.390 --> 00:33:23.490
I definitely think about it
along the lines of where a

00:33:23.490 --> 00:33:25.530
study is progressing. So

00:33:26.460 --> 00:33:29.880
in the early, early phases
of a clinical trial,

00:33:29.880 --> 00:33:34.660
things or even preclinical things
can be much more exploratory.

00:33:35.610 --> 00:33:40.650
And so when exploratory is the
goal, hypothesis generating is the goal,

00:33:42.850 --> 00:33:43.660
go crazy

00:33:45.630 --> 00:33:48.210
and put a disclaimer. [LAUGHS]

00:33:49.290 --> 00:33:53.625
That there are things discoverable
here that need to be

00:33:54.270 --> 00:33:56.880
proven outside of
this application.

00:33:58.200 --> 00:34:01.815
And then when you
work your way down to

00:34:03.180 --> 00:34:05.620
questions that are
more formulated,

00:34:06.960 --> 00:34:09.030
a researcher has
really provided

00:34:09.870 --> 00:34:12.082
the question they're
trying to answer.

00:34:13.500 --> 00:34:14.040
I would--

00:34:14.040 --> 00:34:17.639
that's where I would think
about limiting it and trying to

00:34:17.639 --> 00:34:20.699
really just answer the
question that's being asked,

00:34:20.699 --> 00:34:24.000
and limit the back and forth because

00:34:25.640 --> 00:34:27.570
there's a whole
theory behind this.

00:34:27.570 --> 00:34:30.960
But when you're in phase
3 of a clinical trial,

00:34:30.960 --> 00:34:33.880
the question
should be laid out,

00:34:33.880 --> 00:34:35.760
and the question
should be answerable.

00:34:36.690 --> 00:34:40.570
And that upholds the entire
integrity of the trial.

00:34:40.570 --> 00:34:42.719
So that's kind of
how I think about it.

00:34:43.399 --> 00:34:44.800
AUDIENCE: No, that's
great. Thank you.

00:34:44.800 --> 00:34:47.100
I also really appreciate
the disclaimer.

00:34:48.389 --> 00:34:50.400
STEPHANIE LUSSIER: Yeah,
use disclaimers. [LAUGHS]

00:34:50.400 --> 00:34:50.900
AUDIENCE: Right.

00:34:52.600 --> 00:34:53.040
RACHEL DEMPSEY:

00:34:53.040 --> 00:34:56.699
I see Abigail and Jesse you
had some thoughts on this topic

00:34:56.699 --> 00:34:58.365
too. Do you want
to jump in here?

00:35:01.470 --> 00:35:05.220
I'm not sure if you're in
places where you can jump in or

00:35:05.220 --> 00:35:07.075
not, but I'll give
a second here.

00:35:07.075 --> 00:35:08.870
AUDIENCE: Oh, was this
about interactivity? RACHEL

00:35:08.870 --> 00:35:09.800
DEMPSEY: Yeah.

00:35:09.800 --> 00:35:14.390
- Oh, yeah, no I was
just saying, I don't know, real dashboards--

00:35:15.926 --> 00:35:17.910
dashboards are tough
because people do--

00:35:17.910 --> 00:35:20.670
like you give people self-serve
analytics and then they come to

00:35:20.670 --> 00:35:22.710
conclusions which
are wrong. Sometimes,

00:35:22.710 --> 00:35:25.690
so figuring out how to
manage that I think can--

00:35:25.690 --> 00:35:27.490
I found that to be difficult.

00:35:27.490 --> 00:35:28.650
So sometimes I just
want to give somebody,

00:35:28.650 --> 00:35:31.140
like I wrote you a paper,
I made you a PowerPoint thing

00:35:32.280 --> 00:35:34.800
as useful as the interactivity
definitely can be--

00:35:36.040 --> 00:35:37.645
RACHEL DEMPSEY: Definitely.

00:35:39.150 --> 00:35:41.100
Kevin, I see you had a
question in the chat.

00:35:41.100 --> 00:35:42.120
Want to jump in here?

00:35:43.410 --> 00:35:44.920
AUDIENCE: Sure. Yeah. [LAUGHS]

00:35:46.150 --> 00:35:46.930
What was my question?

00:35:48.430 --> 00:35:48.880
- Sorry.

00:35:48.880 --> 00:35:51.760
I know when there's a lot
of chat it gets hard to remember

00:35:51.760 --> 00:35:52.970
what our questions were.

00:35:52.970 --> 00:35:54.190
AUDIENCE: Yeah, it was about--

00:35:54.190 --> 00:35:57.700
oh, that's right, I remember.
It was about deep work.

00:35:58.210 --> 00:36:01.780
And as a manager,
and the question was basically,

00:36:01.780 --> 00:36:06.520
are you able to manage to carve
out time to do deep work still

00:36:06.520 --> 00:36:08.590
or do you have to
delegate most of that?

00:36:08.590 --> 00:36:10.495
Or how do you kind
of handle that?

00:36:11.175 --> 00:36:12.005
STEPHANIE LUSSIER: Yeah.

00:36:13.340 --> 00:36:13.840
So

00:36:14.390 --> 00:36:18.170
generally, I would say
that my manager workload--

00:36:18.170 --> 00:36:23.990
like workload as a manager is not 40
hours a week in any way right now.

00:36:24.630 --> 00:36:29.620
And so I definitely find time to
carve out for deep work. And I also

00:36:30.320 --> 00:36:34.460
am not overloaded on meetings,
which I know what that

00:36:34.460 --> 00:36:37.685
looks like from previous
experience as well.

00:36:38.640 --> 00:36:40.920
And so it definitely is doable.

00:36:40.920 --> 00:36:44.930
I think part of that is
because our group is so young.

00:36:46.100 --> 00:36:52.520
Everyone is heads down
creating stuff, doing work, focusing,

00:36:53.300 --> 00:36:54.230
and so

00:36:55.460 --> 00:36:59.435
that kind of contributes to
an overall environment where we're all

00:37:01.310 --> 00:37:04.250
getting in hours
for deep work. Yeah.

00:37:05.050 --> 00:37:07.500
- But do you end
up having to hit--

00:37:08.070 --> 00:37:11.970
attend lots of meetings that they don't
have to go to to interface with

00:37:13.410 --> 00:37:16.470
the people that you guys
are serving or other management and

00:37:16.470 --> 00:37:17.550
things like that?

00:37:17.550 --> 00:37:23.130
- Yeah, right now I really
feel like that is being handled

00:37:23.130 --> 00:37:25.920
well. Like the meetings
that I'm attending are

00:37:26.940 --> 00:37:29.030
relevant and helpful.

00:37:30.510 --> 00:37:34.110
And I could see, of course,
how that could change.

00:37:36.000 --> 00:37:36.500
But

00:37:37.110 --> 00:37:38.880
yeah, I think it's
something to always

00:37:39.780 --> 00:37:43.550
keep in the front of your
mind is time resourcing.

00:37:44.880 --> 00:37:46.870
I'm a fan of time blocking

00:37:47.550 --> 00:37:51.250
in my calendar. And so
always asking the question,

00:37:51.250 --> 00:37:52.740
do I need to be
at this meeting?

00:37:52.740 --> 00:37:54.720
What am I getting
out of this meeting?

00:37:54.720 --> 00:37:57.120
And you don't always hit
the nail on the head.

00:37:57.120 --> 00:37:58.335
You go to a meeting that

00:37:59.160 --> 00:38:02.280
you shouldn't have been at or
you miss a meeting that you

00:38:02.280 --> 00:38:03.060
should have been at.

00:38:03.810 --> 00:38:07.795
But just always keeping it
in the forefront of your mind

00:38:08.710 --> 00:38:10.900
I think is an important skill.

00:38:11.740 --> 00:38:13.060
AUDIENCE: All right,
thanks so much. STEPHANIE

00:38:13.060 --> 00:38:13.750
LUSSIER: Yeah. RACHEL

00:38:13.750 --> 00:38:14.020
DEMPSEY:

00:38:14.020 --> 00:38:18.010
There's something I've been thinking
a lot about recently as well.

00:38:18.010 --> 00:38:22.210
Like initially when you get
invited to a meeting with a new

00:38:22.210 --> 00:38:25.180
team it feels good.
Like, oh, I'm--

00:38:25.180 --> 00:38:27.860
they want my point of
view in this conversation.

00:38:27.860 --> 00:38:28.930
And then when I realize, OK,

00:38:28.930 --> 00:38:32.170
that meeting is every
week or every other week.

00:38:33.400 --> 00:38:36.400
At what point can
you then say, OK,

00:38:36.400 --> 00:38:40.975
no I don't think I need to
be at this meeting every week. And

00:38:41.560 --> 00:38:42.910
how do you

00:38:44.040 --> 00:38:47.685
communicate that to people that know
you're just not going to attend? STEPHANIE

00:38:47.685 --> 00:38:48.465
LUSSIER: Yeah.

00:38:50.040 --> 00:38:51.540
I think as

00:38:52.710 --> 00:38:56.420
soon as you're saying
to yourself, is this useful?

00:38:57.120 --> 00:38:58.530
It might not be useful.

00:38:59.680 --> 00:39:05.700
And so just making it a
conversation I think. But it's not easy.

00:39:07.680 --> 00:39:10.990
But yeah, I think
it's something for any

00:39:11.670 --> 00:39:13.580
career person to always

00:39:14.340 --> 00:39:15.285
be thinking about.

00:39:16.470 --> 00:39:22.415
- I see a few ideas here in
the chat. Donald, do you want to jump in?

00:39:22.415 --> 00:39:23.070
AUDIENCE: Sure.

00:39:23.070 --> 00:39:26.190
Just as far as carving out deep
work time I think I had some

00:39:26.190 --> 00:39:27.855
questions too, so I'll
try and answer those.

00:39:28.830 --> 00:39:32.970
I usually block out two to
three hour chunks multiple times a week.

00:39:33.990 --> 00:39:36.210
I think it's also recommended in
boards book to do that too,

00:39:36.210 --> 00:39:38.775
and I've been doing it ever
since and it's been extremely helpful.

00:39:39.490 --> 00:39:42.420
I think Jared you asked if
people ever complain that I'm

00:39:42.420 --> 00:39:47.730
never available. Some times, but
I also notify my director

00:39:47.730 --> 00:39:49.970
who I report to like,
oh, I'm doing this,

00:39:49.970 --> 00:39:51.580
this is really important
because I'm a knowledge worker

00:39:51.580 --> 00:39:52.720
I need this time to do that,

00:39:52.720 --> 00:39:55.990
and he's completely on
board with it. So usually,

00:39:55.990 --> 00:39:57.460
we kind of loop in if we need

00:39:58.180 --> 00:40:01.575
discussion of priorities to see
if anything else needs to happen.

00:40:02.810 --> 00:40:05.650
- I see that Frank
is on the call,

00:40:05.650 --> 00:40:07.270
and I don't mean
to call on you,

00:40:07.270 --> 00:40:10.030
but it's something that's always stuck
with me that you said, Frank,

00:40:10.030 --> 00:40:14.200
is like you color code some of
the meetings that you have on your calendar,

00:40:14.200 --> 00:40:18.190
and the ones that give you
energy are a certain color and

00:40:18.190 --> 00:40:18.730
the ones that are--

00:40:18.730 --> 00:40:19.230
[LAUGHS]

00:40:19.660 --> 00:40:21.610
and maybe I'm putting
words in your mouth,

00:40:21.610 --> 00:40:24.140
but are more draining
a different color.

00:40:24.140 --> 00:40:26.995
And I thought that was a really
interesting approach too.

00:40:27.996 --> 00:40:30.890
STEPHANIE LUSSIER: I hope,
Frank, your calendar doesn't face

00:40:31.930 --> 00:40:34.660
all of your coworkers
in that case.

00:40:34.660 --> 00:40:35.544
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:37.312 --> 00:40:39.850
AUDIENCE: But honestly,
even if it does,

00:40:41.530 --> 00:40:42.790
that's fine with me.

00:40:43.480 --> 00:40:43.980
And

00:40:44.920 --> 00:40:47.170
so most of this came
when I was at Target,

00:40:47.170 --> 00:40:48.670
and Target huge company,

00:40:49.270 --> 00:40:51.410
most of us work in
huge companies. And

00:40:53.860 --> 00:40:54.520
yeah, I mean,

00:40:54.520 --> 00:40:57.220
in those cases I was always
pretty honest about that stuff.

00:40:57.220 --> 00:40:58.960
I was like, hey, yeah,
I'm going to show up.

00:40:58.960 --> 00:41:00.640
Like this is a little
bit draining for me,

00:41:00.640 --> 00:41:02.200
but I know it's important
for me to hear this.

00:41:02.200 --> 00:41:03.850
I know it's important
for me to be here.

00:41:03.850 --> 00:41:04.720
When you do the color

00:41:05.740 --> 00:41:10.030
coding, it makes it very easy to
see like, hey, red, red, red, red, red,

00:41:10.630 --> 00:41:14.870
and then the last red
meeting is something that's really important.

00:41:14.870 --> 00:41:18.430
And you have to be paying
attention and on top of it.

00:41:18.430 --> 00:41:22.540
And I mean, all of us love
to find patterns in data even if

00:41:22.540 --> 00:41:24.865
your sample size
is really small.

00:41:25.630 --> 00:41:28.600
And then being able to
just reflect on that, and notice

00:41:28.600 --> 00:41:30.010
that, oh, man,

00:41:30.010 --> 00:41:33.460
there's no way I'm going to be
able to give my focus, and attention,

00:41:33.460 --> 00:41:36.970
and 100% at like this really
important meeting to the one on

00:41:36.970 --> 00:41:38.275
one with a team member or

00:41:38.980 --> 00:41:40.120
the big meeting with the VP.

00:41:41.230 --> 00:41:43.550
If you can know
how you will be,

00:41:43.550 --> 00:41:46.240
and then sharing that with
other people around you like

00:41:46.240 --> 00:41:49.750
people are surprisingly or
maybe not surprisingly willing

00:41:49.750 --> 00:41:54.220
to adjust. So I've always
been a fan of that visibility.

00:41:55.000 --> 00:41:56.740
If it rubs people
the wrong way,

00:41:56.740 --> 00:41:59.530
it's going to be even worse
for the whole organization if you

00:41:59.530 --> 00:42:01.750
show up to this really
important meeting and you're

00:42:01.750 --> 00:42:04.300
drained. So it's
been my philosophy.

00:42:04.972 --> 00:42:09.240
- OK, I'm thinking of a scatter
plot where we're plotting

00:42:10.132 --> 00:42:11.850
importance versus

00:42:12.630 --> 00:42:16.410
passion, maybe. And
so really important,

00:42:16.410 --> 00:42:18.615
no passion kind of
still got to go.

00:42:19.470 --> 00:42:23.550
Less important tons of passion
when there's time for it.

00:42:24.180 --> 00:42:25.860
Like we have quadrants.

00:42:25.860 --> 00:42:28.155
Is that along the lines
of your thought process?

00:42:28.155 --> 00:42:31.365
- Yeah, but the way

00:42:32.010 --> 00:42:34.605
you describe that and the
way I'm visualizing it, like

00:42:35.340 --> 00:42:36.975
none of this stuff
lives in a silo.

00:42:37.830 --> 00:42:41.100
Like it's all connected and
especially the series of

00:42:41.100 --> 00:42:43.680
meetings leading up to
something important before you

00:42:43.680 --> 00:42:46.860
can go on a walk if
you're in Minneapolis and in a Skyway,

00:42:46.860 --> 00:42:49.140
and like re
re-energize yourself.

00:42:50.080 --> 00:42:51.210
So I'm not exactly sure,

00:42:51.210 --> 00:42:54.490
maybe we can put together a
three dimensional scatter plot,

00:42:54.490 --> 00:42:58.560
so we can see these things.
Like time would be your other axis. STEPHANIE

00:42:58.560 --> 00:43:00.480
LUSSIER: Time of
day is important,

00:43:00.480 --> 00:43:03.120
and that's your
third dimension? OK.

00:43:03.120 --> 00:43:03.510
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:43:03.510 --> 00:43:04.020
- Very cool.

00:43:04.020 --> 00:43:04.744
- [LAUGHS]

00:43:06.440 --> 00:43:07.850
We should write that one up.

00:43:07.850 --> 00:43:08.350
- Yeah.

00:43:09.222 --> 00:43:11.650
RACHEL DEMPSEY: Mike, I
see you had a question

00:43:12.410 --> 00:43:13.720
earlier on

00:43:16.910 --> 00:43:20.510
as a consultant when somebody asks,
can you help me do x?

00:43:20.510 --> 00:43:23.480
Do you want to jump in and
continue that question?

00:43:23.480 --> 00:43:23.720
-

00:43:23.720 --> 00:43:26.360
I think that was very
much related to what you were

00:43:26.360 --> 00:43:27.860
talking about
previously on the-- RACHEL

00:43:27.860 --> 00:43:28.115
DEMPSEY: OK.

00:43:28.115 --> 00:43:31.400
AUDIENCE: --why is someone
asking for a report?

00:43:32.720 --> 00:43:33.580
But it

00:43:34.340 --> 00:43:35.990
would be interesting to
hear what Stephanie thinks.

00:43:35.990 --> 00:43:39.800
That sometimes I get these
messages that go, Mike, can you

00:43:39.800 --> 00:43:41.600
get this package for me?

00:43:41.600 --> 00:43:43.445
Or could you show me
how to do that thing?

00:43:44.490 --> 00:43:46.660
Whether any context. And

00:43:47.340 --> 00:43:49.690
as a consultant, you
might think, well,

00:43:49.690 --> 00:43:51.360
I'll just jump
in and I'll help.

00:43:51.360 --> 00:43:54.720
I'll just do it help
them get that thing.

00:43:54.720 --> 00:43:59.040
But then if you unpick it
you realize that actually there's a

00:43:59.040 --> 00:44:04.410
whole world of either there's
a better solution or there's a

00:44:04.410 --> 00:44:08.070
simpler thing that they
could do or it's related to

00:44:08.700 --> 00:44:10.470
back to the why question.

00:44:12.768 --> 00:44:13.268
So

00:44:15.850 --> 00:44:17.670
you can be the
helpful consultant,

00:44:17.670 --> 00:44:20.310
or you can actually
do something that makes--

00:44:21.030 --> 00:44:24.310
that eliminates
tech debt or makes

00:44:25.230 --> 00:44:27.742
it's easier to
maintain or something.

00:44:27.742 --> 00:44:28.100
STEPHANIE LUSSIER: Yeah,

00:44:28.100 --> 00:44:33.665
I think I'm coming to mind
with examples just within my own team

00:44:34.280 --> 00:44:36.650
of getting a ping saying,

00:44:37.940 --> 00:44:38.630
how do I--

00:44:38.630 --> 00:44:42.680
Stephanie, how do I make
this access log 10? And

00:44:43.820 --> 00:44:47.570
it's probably a good idea to
first ask why are you making

00:44:47.570 --> 00:44:49.715
that access log 10?
These kinds of things,

00:44:50.630 --> 00:44:53.510
to try to make sure
you're solving the right

00:44:54.260 --> 00:44:56.270
question. Yeah, I like that.

